1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:02,960
People are quietly replacing their

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:04,627
phone keyboards with their voice.

3
00:00:04,867 --> 00:00:07,697
Apps like Wispr Flow turn rambling speech into clean,

4
00:00:07,697 --> 00:00:10,081
ready-to-send text, in any app.

5
00:00:10,321 --> 00:00:13,191
Here's who leads on iPhone, why people get hooked,

6
00:00:13,191 --> 00:00:16,702
and how a Swedish version could run entirely on the phone.

7
00:00:17,352 --> 00:00:18,562
First, the plumbing:

8
00:00:18,562 --> 00:00:21,229
iOS won't let a keyboard use the microphone.

9
00:00:21,469 --> 00:00:25,039
So every voice keyboard is two parts: a thin keyboard,

10
00:00:25,039 --> 00:00:27,344
and a main app that records in the background.

11
00:00:27,584 --> 00:00:30,782
Starting a session means a trip to that app,

12
00:00:30,782 --> 00:00:34,423
and since iOS 26.4, users must swipe back by hand.

13
00:00:35,073 --> 00:00:35,946
So who's winning?

14
00:00:36,186 --> 00:00:37,946
Wispr Flow is the reference,

15
00:00:37,946 --> 00:00:41,487
with 4.8 stars from about seventeen thousand ratings.

16
00:00:41,727 --> 00:00:44,587
9to5Mac called Aqua Voice the best dictation

17
00:00:44,587 --> 00:00:46,997
app its reviewer had ever used,

18
00:00:46,997 --> 00:00:50,101
and Make Tech Easier called Willow the most accurate.

19
00:00:50,341 --> 00:00:53,591
Superwhisper, Typeless and Monologue round out the field,

20
00:00:53,591 --> 00:00:55,280
nearly all of them in the cloud.

21
00:00:55,930 --> 00:00:58,464
The most interesting ideas come from the edges.

22
00:00:58,704 --> 00:01:00,334
Aqua lets you edit by voice:

23
00:01:00,334 --> 00:01:02,812
make this shorter, translate it, spell a name.

24
00:01:03,052 --> 00:01:05,062
Typeless keeps only what you meant when

25
00:01:05,062 --> 00:01:06,957
you change your mind mid-sentence.

26
00:01:07,197 --> 00:01:10,767
And small apps like Outspoken and KeySay already run

27
00:01:10,767 --> 00:01:13,699
transcription and AI cleanup entirely on the phone.

28
00:01:14,349 --> 00:01:16,989
Ranked by leverage, one feature dominates:

29
00:01:16,989 --> 00:01:18,458
text you don't have to edit.

30
00:01:18,698 --> 00:01:20,398
Punctuation, filler words,

31
00:01:20,398 --> 00:01:22,583
self-corrections and lists, all handled.

32
00:01:22,823 --> 00:01:26,853
Then come one-tap capture anywhere, getting names and jargon right,

33
00:01:26,853 --> 00:01:29,132
speed you can feel, and editing by voice.

34
00:01:29,782 --> 00:01:31,101
Why do people get hooked?

35
00:01:31,341 --> 00:01:34,172
Speaking is about three times faster than thumb-typing.

36
00:01:34,412 --> 00:01:36,992
Wispr says eighty-nine percent of its messages go

37
00:01:36,992 --> 00:01:40,091
out without an edit, sent in about half a second.

38
00:01:40,331 --> 00:01:43,050
People stop proofreading and start thinking out loud.

39
00:01:43,290 --> 00:01:45,480
Wispr reports seventy percent of

40
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:46,931
users still active after a year.

41
00:01:47,171 --> 00:01:48,513
Trust turns into habit.

42
00:01:49,163 --> 00:01:52,526
Can it all run locally? Yes, but not inside the keyboard.

43
00:01:52,766 --> 00:01:55,646
iOS kills a keyboard at around seventy megabytes,

44
00:01:55,646 --> 00:01:57,724
so the models run in the main app.

45
00:01:57,964 --> 00:02:01,074
There, the GPU is off limits, and from iOS 27

46
00:02:01,074 --> 00:02:04,899
the Neural Engine needs a special entitlement.

47
00:02:05,139 --> 00:02:09,809
On the CPU, Gemma 4 E2B writes about twenty-five tokens a second,

48
00:02:09,809 --> 00:02:12,624
so a full rewrite takes about four seconds.

49
00:02:12,864 --> 00:02:14,924
Stream it into the text field instead,

50
00:02:14,924 --> 00:02:16,993
and words appear in about half a second.

51
00:02:17,643 --> 00:02:19,131
And Swedish is wide open.

52
00:02:19,371 --> 00:02:23,092
Apple's Swedish dictation adds no punctuation,

53
00:02:23,092 --> 00:02:26,813
and its new AI dictation in iOS 27 is English only.

54
00:02:27,053 --> 00:02:30,183
Klang's Pianissimo model reaches about four and a half

55
00:02:30,183 --> 00:02:33,268
percent word error rate in Swedish, on the device.

56
00:02:33,508 --> 00:02:36,008
Add cleanup that understands Swedish fillers,

57
00:02:36,008 --> 00:02:38,068
split compounds and number formats,

58
00:02:38,068 --> 00:02:40,576
and you have something nobody offers today.

59
00:02:41,226 --> 00:02:43,466
The plan: nail edit-free Swedish text,

60
00:02:43,466 --> 00:02:46,680
make sessions painless, and learn each user's words.

61
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:48,982
Then add voice editing and translation.

62
00:02:49,222 --> 00:02:50,742
Track one number above all:

63
00:02:50,742 --> 00:02:53,097
how often a message goes out untouched.

64
00:02:53,337 --> 00:02:55,117
Talk, don't type. In Swedish.

65
00:02:55,117 --> 00:02:56,531
With nothing leaving the phone.
